 what's challenging is our  design systems are actually about the  collaborative network and we're trying  to get to that while we're existing in  this extremely centralized model so  perhaps to me one of the most powerful  things that our design systems do when  they're introduced into an organization  is they become one of the first  manifestations of a team that is trying  to work across all of these silos and  face it that is really hard because our  structures aren't set up  that so you're doing an amazing job if  you're pushing this through so these are  two articles that came out a couple  years ago one is my Nathan Curtis and  the other one is a response by Gina and  Nathan is looking at various models for  how to scale a design system and he  talks about three he says there's this  sort of solitary Overlord model that  there's one person that makes the design  system and they're super excited and  there's hyper focus on their team's  needs  I was definitely that person for a while  it's easy to be that person if you push  this thing forward there's a centralized  pattern which is a single central design  team that produces and supports the  system and everyone else is using it and  then there's this concept of a federated  pattern so designers from multiple teams  come decide on the system together and  while it is always best I think to aim  for a type of cross-functional team  where we're really pulling the people  that we need from the different  departments to make our design systems I  think that if we take this idea of a  design system as a facilitator it's also  okay if you happen to be that solitary  person working on your design system  what that means is that you need to  design all of your processes to be  incredibly open and to go out into the  organization and to find out what the  people actually need so you're really  going to be not only the developer of  the design system but a UX researcher  team of one which is really hard but  that is a 